            <ArticleTitle>Evaluation of suspected renal colic patients with unenhanced low-dose multi-detector computed tomography.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>190-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To determine the proportion of correctly diagnosed ureteric calculi and alternate diagnoses using unenhanced low dose multidetector CT KUB.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Descriptive - prospective study.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Aga Khan University Hospital, Nairobi between November 2006 and October 2007.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUBJECTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">One hundred and four patients underwent low dose multi-detector computed tomography (MDCT) KUB.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Ninety (47 males and 43 females with age range of 17 - 79 years, mean 40 years) were included in the statistical analysis while 14 were excluded. Eighteen out of ninety (20%) were classified as definitely having ureteric calculi on the composite reference. Among the 18,17 were correctly identified on unenhanced low dose MDCT, giving a proportion of (17/18) 94.4%, Pearson Correlation of 0.898 and Fishers exact test significance &lt; 0.001. 29/90 (32%) had alternative diagnosis and 42/90 (47%) had no abnormality detected on unenhanced low dose MDCT.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">This study shows that, unenhanced low dose MDCT KUB can be effectively used for evaluation of suspected renal colic patients as demonstrated by the statistically significant correctly detected ureteric calculi.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
